>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/research" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Current Work</a></p>
    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-2">
      Collaboration · DFKI
    </div>
    <h1>Prompt framing and latent control states in language models</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] italic !text-lg !mb-3">
      When a change in prompt framing changes a model’s answer, does it also change a causally relevant
      internal state — or only the output?
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-dim)] !mb-8">
      A bounded mechanistic-interpretability collaboration with DFKI. My role on the project is the
      experimental design.
    </p>

    <p>
      Prompt framing is known to move a model’s answers. On its own, that is compatible with a purely
      output-level effect: the model could reach the same internal decision and merely phrase it
      differently. This project asks the causal version of the question instead — whether framing also
      moves an internal state that is responsible for the decision, not just the words that come out.
    </p>

    <h2>The design</h2>
    <p>
      The setup is a utilitarian minimal-pair experiment. Each pair holds the moral scenario fixed and
      varies one thing at a time — the dilemma, the size of the benefit at stake, and the framing of the
      prompt — so a difference in the model’s decision can be traced to a specific manipulation rather
      than to unrelated content. Keeping the pair matched is what lets an internal difference be read as
      caused by framing rather than by a change of topic.
    </p>

    <h2>The test</h2>
    <p>
      The evidence chain is deliberately minimal and direct. Rather than a full circuit analysis, it
      targets one question: can the direction in the model’s activations that tracks framing be added or
      removed, and does doing so change the decision? A necessity-and-sufficiency check of that kind is
      enough to separate “framing changes a causally relevant state” from “framing changes only the
      answer.” Heavier machinery — exhaustive activation patching, attention-head localization, sparse
      dictionaries — stays out of scope unless the minimal chain turns out to need it.
    </p>

    <h2>Scope and limits</h2>
    <p>
      This is a focused contribution, not an open-ended interpretability program: a minimal, credible
      mechanistic-evidence chain with its limitations stated plainly. The aim is to answer the causal
      question at the smallest scale that is convincing, and to be explicit about what a single
      steering-style intervention can and cannot show about a model’s internal states.
    </p>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p class="cw-status">
      The experiment is designed and the minimal evidence chain is being assembled. Any claim will be
      held to what the intervention actually licenses, with the limits reported rather than rounded away.
    </p>

    <p class="!mt-10 !text-sm !text-[var(--color-text-dim)]">
      <a href="/research" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Back to Current Work</a>
    </p>
